  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nH5 height rises will continue today as mid-level ridge axis moves \neastward. Strong dAVA will continue to support the development of a \nbroad surface anticyclone centered over the Great Lakes Region and \nnorthern Ohio River Valley. The subsidence will continue to provide \nmostly clear skies, perhaps with some high-level cirrus passing \noverhead. Low-level flow turns southeasterly today resulting in \nmodest WAA across the region. This will push afternoon highs into \nthe mid and upper 50s. Stronger pressure gradient and mixing this \nafternoon may allow for southeastern wind gusts around 15 MPH today. \nThis pattern will continue through Tuesday and Wednesday, with the \nmain H5 ridge axis passing east of the area early Wednesday morning, \nbut providing continued WAA with south to southwesterly lower \ntropospheric flow. Wednesday and Thursday will see temperatures push \ninto the mid and upper 60s, with perhaps some lower 70s for areas \nsouth of U.S. Hwy. 50 on Thursday afternoon.  \n\nCompact short-wave trough with notable vorticity maxima lifts from \nthe Rockies into the Northern Plains on Thursday with attendant \nsurface cyclone tracking northeastward, and a weak trailing cold \nfront. The bulk of the forcing and flow associated with this system \nwill miss our forecast area, but if we get enough moisture \ntransport, there may be just enough lift to generate some isolated \nshower activity at some point on Thursday into Friday. But current \nensemble suites keep precipitation probabilities under 20 percent \nfor the vast majority of the region as this feature passes through. \n\nA stronger PV anomaly with deeper mid-level trough and several vort \nmaxima will start pushing across the Rockies on Friday and bring \nmore forcing into the Plains and lower Missouri Valley by Saturday. \nDeterministic guidance is depicting that the trough will start to \nlift northeastward after entering the High Plains, but even though \nthe center of the surface cyclone moves more northeastward, surface \npressure falls will continue across the area for most of Saturday \nand Sunday, along with better moisture transport ahead of the \ntrialing cold front. Rain shower activity is favored over the \nweekend with this system. While the stronger flow will be providing \nplenty of lift and forcing, the instability parameter space remains \nweak both in deterministic and ensemble guidance. Most members of \nthe GEFS for example do not bring MUCAPE values any higher than 500 \nJ/kg. The threat for strong thunderstorms at this point seems \nminimal, though there will a lot of background shear available with \nthis feature. Instability trends heading into the weekend will need \nto be monitored. The cold front associated with this system passes \nthrough Saturday Night and will lead to more seasonable temperatures \non Sunday and for the start of the next work week.",
